content.text:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:

   The user made exactly one request: **"Add an enemy that patrols the level, chases the player when it sees them, and holds a rifle in its right hand."**

   Scope taken: patrol behaviour, sight-based chase, and a rifle held in the right hand. Enemy *firing* was deliberately excluded — it was not asked for, and an enemy that damages the player would break `playtests/shooter.playtest.json`'s exact assertions (`health equals 65` at the "damage" step, `deaths equals 1` at "death"). The rifle instead aims at the player when alerted (visual only). This decision must be reported to the user.

   Working directory: `/home/joao/projects/threenative/threenative-engine/.worktrees/prd-157-capability-discovery/docs/verification/.prd157-baseline-work/run-1` — a generated ThreeNative arena-shooter ("run-1"). Everything in it is untracked in git, so there is no baseline diff available.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - ThreeNative framework: `@threenative/core` (0.2.0), `@threenative/physics` (0.2.1), `@threenative/ui`, `@threenative/playtest` (0.2.0), `three` 0.185.1.
   - Godot-borrowed vocabulary: `CharacterBody3D.moveAndSlide(dt)`, `RigidBody3D`, `Area3D`, `CollisionShape3D`, `PhysicsDirectSpaceState3D.intersectRay/intersectShape/intersectPoint`.
   - Bulk physics step: `moveAndSlide` *queues* motion; `writeKinematic` serialises the object transform into a Float32Array before the step, `applyTransform` writes the result back. `THREE.Vector3` is mutable, so `mesh.position` aliases the live transform.
   - Collision layers (from `src/entities/Player.ts`): `WORLD_LAYER=1`, `PLAYER_LAYER=2`, `HOSTILE_LAYER=4`, `FRIENDLY_LAYER=8`, `PROJECTILE_LAYER=16`.
   - `CollisionShape3D.capsule(halfHeight, radius)` is centred on the body origin; a rig must be offset down by `halfHeight + radius` or it floats.
   - Playtest harness: `components` assertions read `entity.debug()`; visibility reads `entity.mesh ?? entity.object`; `observeEntity` samples the entity's **local** `object.quaternion`. Assertions are "fail closed" and reject trivially-satisfied invariants (`triviality: reject-initial-value`).
   - TypeScript 5.9 strict, ESM-only, `.js` extensions on relative imports, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `verbatimModuleSyntax`, interfaces `I`-prefixed, 100-column formatting (no biome config in this generated project).
   - Project rule (from the generated `CLAUDE.md`): when a framework API blocks you, drop to plain portable Three.js for that one thing, keep the fallback portable (no `document`/`window`/`localStorage`/dynamic `import()`/raw physics handles), and **report the API, what you expected, what happened, and what you wrote instead**.
   - Project rule: hitscan/radius/scans must use `ctx.physics.directSpaceState`, never `ctx.raycast()` or a JS distance scan.
   - Project rule: `playtests/survives.playtest.json` is the durable smoke proof; `shooter`/`fail` are this arena's examples.

3. Files and Code Sections:

   - **`src/render/shapes.ts`** (modified) — added `Object3D` to the `three` import and appended the rifleman visual. Heading is intentionally NOT a handle; it was moved onto `root` because the playtest bridge observes `entity.mesh.quaternion`:
     ```ts
     export interface IRiflemanVisual {
       readonly root: Group;   // rotate about Y to face a heading; local forward -Z, right hand +X
       readonly aim: Group;    // right shoulder pivot; positive rotation.x lifts the muzzle
       readonly muzzle: Object3D;
       readonly stride: (phase: number, amount: number) => void;
     }
     export const RIFLEMAN_EYE_HEIGHT = 1.66;
     function pivot(parent: Group, x: number, y: number, z: number): Group { ... }
     function limb(parent, width, height, depth, material, y, z = 0): Mesh { ... }
     function createRifle(materials: ShooterMaterials): { group: Group; muzzle: Object3D } { ... }
     export function createRiflemanVisual(materials: ShooterMaterials, seat: number): IRiflemanVisual {
       const root = new Group(); root.name = "rifleman";
       const body = pivot(root, 0, -seat, 0); body.name = "rifleman-body";
       const hipLeft = pivot(body, -0.17, 0.76, 0);
       const hipRight = pivot(body, 0.17, 0.76, 0);
       ... torso/head/visor/legs from materials.hostile, trim, shadow ...
       const shoulderLeft = pivot(body, -0.4, 1.42, 0);
       const aim = pivot(body, 0.4, 1.42, 0);   // +X = right hand
       const rifle = createRifle(materials);
       rifle.group.position.set(0, -0.26, -0.42);
       aim.add(rifle.group);
       const stride = (phase: number, amount: number): void => {
         const swing = Math.sin(phase) * 0.5 * amount;
         hipLeft.rotation.x = swing; hipRight.rotation.x = -swing;
         shoulderLeft.rotation.x = -swing * 0.7;
       };
       stride(0, 0);
       return { aim, muzzle: rifle.muzzle, root, stride };
     }
     ```
     Yaw derivation used throughout: for a visual whose local forward is `-Z`, `rotation.y = Math.atan2(-x, -z)`; facing vector back out is `(-sin(yaw), 0, -cos(yaw))`.

   - **`src/entities/Enemy.ts`** (new) — the patrol/chase rifleman. Constants: `HALF_HEIGHT=0.45`, `RADIUS=0.38`, `SEAT=0.83`, `EYE = new Vector3(0, RIFLEMAN_EYE_HEIGHT - SEAT, 0)` = `(0,0.83,0)`, `PATROL_SPEED=2`, `CHASE_SPEED=3.4`, `TURN_RATE=4.6`, `ARRIVAL=0.7`, `STANDOFF=2.6`, `SIGHT_RANGE=14`, `SIGHT_COS=cos(50°)`, `SIGHT_INTERVAL=0.12`, `MEMORY=2.5`, `REST_PITCH=-0.5`. Helpers `approachAngle(current, target, maxDelta)` (wrap-safe via `atan2(sin Δ, cos Δ)`) and `headingAngle(x, z)`.
     ```ts
     export type EnemyMode = "patrol" | "chase" | "search";
     export interface IEnemyOptions {
       readonly route: readonly Vector3[];
       readonly playerBodyId: number;
       readonly playerEye: () => Vector3 | undefined;  // undefined = not a target (dead)
       readonly health?: number;
       readonly leash?: number;
       readonly onAlert?: (alerted: boolean) => void;
       readonly onWaypoint?: (reached: number) => void;
     }
     ```
     Body: `CharacterBody3D` with `collisionLayer: HOSTILE_LAYER`, `collisionMask: WORLD_LAYER | PLAYER_LAYER`, `autostep: { maxHeight: 0.25, minWidth: 0.18 }`, `shape: CollisionShape3D.capsule(0.45, 0.38)`. `#post` = route centroid with `y=0`; `#leash` default 5.
     `update(ctx, dt)`: decrement `#sightTimer`, run `#look(ctx)` every 0.12 s, then `#drive(this.#destination(eye), speed, dt)`, `#cover(eye, moving, dt)`, `this.body.moveAndSlide(dt)`, advance `#stridePhase` and call `#visual.stride`.
     `#look` sets `#range`, and on sight records `#lastSeen`, resets `#memory = MEMORY`, sets `#alertRange` once, updates `#closed = max(#closed, #alertRange - #range)`, and transitions to `"chase"` firing `#onAlert(true)`; otherwise chase→search (firing `#onAlert(false)`) then decays `#memory` and falls back to `"patrol"` with `#waypoint = #nearestWaypoint()`.
     `#canSee`: range check → cone check against `#facing()` → single `directSpaceState(ctx.physics).intersectRay({ collisionMask: WORLD_LAYER | PLAYER_LAYER, from, to: eye })`, true only when `hit?.body.id === this.#playerBodyId`.
     `#destination`: patrol → current waypoint; chase → `undefined` when within `STANDOFF`, else `#leashed(playerEye)`; search → `#leashed(#lastSeen)`.
     `#drive`: zeroes planar velocity, steers at `speed`, counts a waypoint and advances the index when within `ARRIVAL` in patrol mode, and calls `#turnTowards`.
     `#cover`: rests the rifle at `REST_PITCH` in patrol, otherwise turns toward the target when stationary and pitches `aim.rotation.x` to `atan2(target.y - muzzle.y, planarDistance)`.
     Current (buggy) rotation code:
     ```ts
     #turnTowards(x: number, z: number, dt: number): void {
       if (x === 0 && z === 0) return;
       const current = this.mesh.rotation.y;
       this.mesh.rotation.y = approachAngle(current, headingAngle(x, z), TURN_RATE * dt);
     }
     #facing(): Vector3 {
       const yaw = this.mesh.rotation.y;
       return new Vector3(-Math.sin(yaw), 0, -Math.cos(yaw));
     }
     ```
     `takeDamage(amount): boolean` mirrors `Target` (returns true on the killing blow). `debug()` returns `{ alerted, alive, closedTenths, health, mode, position, rangeTenths, waypointsReached }`. `tags = ["hostile", "enemy", "rifleman"]`. `muzzleOrigin()` returns the world muzzle tip.

   - **`src/combat/damage.ts`** (rewritten) — widened to a structural interface so one hitscan/projectile/blast reaches drones and the rifleman alike:
     ```ts
     export interface IDamageable {
       readonly alive: boolean;
       readonly mesh: { readonly position: Vector3 };
       takeDamage(amount: number): boolean;
     }
     export function applyDirectDamage(targets: ReadonlyMap<number, IDamageable>, bodyId: number, amount: number): boolean
     export function applyRadiusDamage(physics, centre, radius, amount, targets: ReadonlyMap<number, IDamageable>): Set<number>
     ```
     This was required, not cosmetic: `applyRadiusDamage` queries `HOSTILE_LAYER` and would have found the enemy then silently failed the `Map<number, Target>` lookup.

   - **`src/state.ts`** — added `enemyAlerted: number;` after `demoTargetAlive`.

   - **`src/scenes/Play.ts`** — new constants and wiring:
     ```ts
     const PLAYER_EYE = new Vector3(0, 0.5, 0);   // inside the capsule so a sight ray lands
     const PATROL_ROUTE = [
       new Vector3(-8.5, SPAWN.y, -9), new Vector3(-8.5, SPAWN.y, 2),
       new Vector3(-6, SPAWN.y, 2),    new Vector3(-6, SPAWN.y, -9),
     ];
     const damageable = new Map<number, IDamageable>();   // alongside the existing targets map
     const enemy = new Enemy(ctx, materials, {
       leash: 5,
       onAlert: (alerted) => emitPlaytestEvent({ entity: "enemy", name: alerted ? "alerted" : "lost-sight" }),
       onWaypoint: (reached) => emitPlaytestEvent({ entity: "enemy", name: "patrol-leg", reached }),
       playerBodyId: player.body.body.id,
       playerEye: () => (player.dead ? undefined : player.mesh.position.clone().add(PLAYER_EYE)),
       route: PATROL_ROUTE,
     });
     damageable.set(enemy.body.body.id, enemy);
     ctx.entities.add("enemy", enemy);
     ```
     Also: `registerTarget` now sets both maps; `removeDeadTargets` deletes from both; `fireHitscan` uses `const killed = applyDirectDamage(damageable, hit.body.id, 40)` and emits `{entity:"enemy", name:"defeated"}` when the enemy dies; `fireProjectile` and `fireRadius` pass `damageable`; `syncStateAndHud` sets `enemyAlerted: enemy.alerted ? 1 : 0`; the frame loop calls `enemy.update(frameCtx, dt)` right after `player.update`.
     Route rationale (documented in the file): the beat runs down the west side, clear of the demo props and of the x≈0 lane the player shoots down, so a patrol never wanders into a test shot. Post = centroid `(-7.25, -3.5)`, leash 5 → closest approach x = -2.25.

   - **`src/render/ui.ts`** — added `hud.copy.alert = "rifleman has you"` and `hud.alert = "shooter-phase shooter-phase--danger"`.
   - **`src/ui/Hud.tsx`** — added `const alerted = useGameState(game, (state) => state.enemyAlerted);` and `{alerted === 1 && <div className={shooterUi.hud.alert}>{shooterUi.hud.copy.alert}</div>}`.

   - **`playtests/enemy.playtest.json`** (new) — `warmupFrames: 10`, `subject: "enemy"`, steps `{wait "patrol" 40 ticks}` then `{wait "chase" 260 ticks}`; asserts components `alerted gte 1 changed`, `closedTenths gte 50 changed`, `mode atSteps [patrol→"patrol", chase→"chase"]`; movement `{entity:"enemy", minDistance:6, pathLength:8, rotationChanged:true, closesDistanceToPosition:{position:[0,0.85,5], min:5}}`; signal `alerted minCount 1`; visibility `enemy minProjectedPixels 20`; diagnostics with the same `runtimeDiagnosticsOptOutReason` pattern `shooter.playtest.json` uses for the known headless-Linux WebGPU buffer diagnostic.

   - **`vite.config.ts`** (out-of-scope repair) — removed `import { createWebBrandPlugin } from "create-threenative"` and its use, with an explanatory comment. Needed because the dev server could not start at all.

4. Errors and fixes:

   - **Three pre-existing typecheck errors**, unrelated to this feature and left alone: `src/scenes/Play.ts(93,41)` `GameCtx` not assignable to `LoadingHost` (`viewport.safeArea` missing), `threenative.config.ts(9,5)` unknown property `icons`, `vite.config.ts(3,10)` no exported member `createWebBrandPlugin`. After all my edits, `npx tsc --noEmit` reports **only** these three.
   - **`$PORT` not substituted by the playtest runner** → `vite --host 127.0.0.1 --port --strictPort` → `CACError: option \`--port <port>\` value is missing`, surfaced as `TN_PLAYTEST_SERVER_FAILED`. Fixed by starting the dev server manually on port 5273 and passing `--url http://127.0.0.1:5273`.
   - **The generated project's dev server would not start at all.** `vite.config.ts` imports `createWebBrandPlugin` from `create-threenative`, but `create-threenative@0.2.2` exports only `cliHelp, createProject, discoverKitManifests, discoverTemplateNames, kitManifest, parseArgs, templateRoot` — there is no brand plugin anywhere in the published package. This is a pre-existing scaffold/engine bug that made the project unrunnable on web before any of my changes. I dropped the plugin from `vite.config.ts` to unblock verification; the server then returned HTTP 200. **This must be reported to the user as a separate, pre-existing breakage I had to repair.**
   - **`movement.facesMovementWithinDegrees` is unsupported by this runner**: `TN_PLAYTEST_OBSERVATION_UNAVAILABLE — Assertion 'movement.facesMovementWithinDegrees' requires observation 'effectLog', but this runner does not produce it.` Removed it and substituted `rotationChanged: true`.
   - **My own wrong assumption about the quaternion round-trip.** I put the heading on `Enemy.mesh` (the root the physics body owns) on the theory that `CharacterBody3D.writeKinematic` → rapier → `applyTransform` would preserve it. The playtest reported `movement.rotation: { rotationDelta: 0 }`, and the rapier backend source confirms why: in `step()`, for `entry.type === "character"` it calls only `entry.body.setNextKinematicTranslation({...})` and **never applies the input rotation**, so `readVisibleTransforms` reads back the body's creation rotation (identity) and `applyTransform` writes identity onto `object.quaternion` every step. This is an **engine bug in `@threenative/physics`**: `CharacterBody3D.writeKinematic` serialises a rotation the rapier backend silently discards. Consequence: `#facing()` is pinned to `-Z`, the vision cone points the wrong way, and the state machine thrashes.

5. Problem Solving:

   Solved: reusing the existing weapon plumbing via a structural `IDamageable`; seating the figure correctly on its capsule (`seat = halfHeight + radius`); putting the LOS aim point inside the player capsule (world y 1.35, not the visor at 1.73 which is above the capsule top of 1.68); choosing a patrol route and leash radius that provably keep the enemy out of the player's x≈0 firing lane so `shooter.playtest.json`'s exact-geometry assertions survive; deciding not to arm the enemy for the same reason; splitting HUD state (`ctx.state.enemyAlerted`) from playtest telemetry (`Enemy.debug()`); getting the project to run at all.

   Latest playtest run (`sh ../../../../scripts/xvfb.sh npx threenative-playtest --scenario "playtests/enemy.playtest.json" --url http://127.0.0.1:5273 --browser-recipe webgpu --headed`):
   - PASS `signal.alerted` count 1; `movement.distance` 8.92 (min 6); `movement.pathLength` 9.59 (min 8); `movement.closesDistance` decrease 8.80 (min 5); `visibility.enemy` 6354 projected pixels; `mode` at step "patrol" = `"patrol"`.
   - FAIL `movement.rotation` — `rotationDelta: 0`.
   - FAIL `component.enemy.alerted` — before 0, after 0.
   - FAIL `component.enemy.closedTenths` — after 18 (1.8 m closed while actually seeing the player) vs required 50.
   - FAIL `mode` at step "chase" — got `"search"`, expected `"chase"`.
   - FAIL `diagnostics` — `consoleErrors: 1` (message text not yet captured; the `consoleMessages`/`console` keys were absent from the result JSON, whose keys are `after, before, diagnostics, diagnosticsPolicy, distance, entity, expectMoved, frames, movementDelta, pathLength, observations, artifactDirectory, capture, assertionResults, pass, runtime, scenario, target, url`).

   Interpretation: the enemy walks its route and closes 8.8 m on the player, and it is on screen — but it acquires the player once at ~14 m, loses sight almost immediately, and thrashes chase↔search, because `#facing()` reads a rotation physics keeps resetting to identity.

6. All user messages:
   - "Add an enemy that patrols the level, chases the player when it sees them, and holds a rifle in its right hand."
   - (Final turn) An instruction to produce this summary with text only and no tool calls.

   No other user-role messages. Everything else in the transcript was system-injected context (project `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` contents, the always-on ADHD-mode hook ruleset, the memory index, background-task notifications, file-modification notices, and task-tool reminders). Note the standing session directive in the environment preamble: **"Do not call the AgentTool unless the user requested it"** and **"Do not use workflows or deep-research unless the user requested it"** — neither was requested. Also standing: registry commands use the repository-local `.npmrc`, which must stay ignored and untracked and whose contents must never be printed.

7. Pending Tasks:
   - Fix the heading so the vision cone works, given that `CharacterBody3D` cannot carry a game-set rotation.
   - Get `playtests/enemy.playtest.json` green, adjusting assertions that the harness cannot support (`rotationChanged` on a character-body root) rather than weakening real claims.
   - Identify the 1 console error the run reported.
   - Re-run `playtests/survives.playtest.json` and `playtests/shooter.playtest.json` to prove the new enemy did not break the existing arena contract (this is the whole reason for the west-side route and the leash, and it is still unverified).
   - Report to the user: the enemy does not fire (and why), the `vite.config.ts` / `createWebBrandPlugin` scaffold breakage I had to repair, the `$PORT` substitution failure, the unsupported `facesMovementWithinDegrees` assertion, and the `@threenative/physics` `CharacterBody3D` rotation-discard engine bug — per the project rule to report the API, what was expected, what happened, and what was written instead.

8. Current Work:

   I was diagnosing the `movement.rotation: { rotationDelta: 0 }` failure by reading the rapier backend in `node_modules/@threenative/physics/dist/index.js` around the `step()` implementation (lines ~380–430). The decisive code:
   ```js
   if (entry.type === "character") {
     ...
     controller.computeColliderMovement(entry.collider, desired, ...);
     const movement = controller.computedMovement();
     entry.body.setNextKinematicTranslation({
       x: current.x + movement.x, y: current.y + movement.y, z: current.z + movement.z
     });
   } else { ... }
   ```
   The `rotation` object is unpacked from the input buffer at offsets +4..+7 but is **never applied for character bodies**. Combined with `readVisibleTransforms` reading `entry.body.rotation()` and `CharacterBody3D.applyTransform` doing `this.object.quaternion.set(qx,qy,qz,qw)`, the enemy root's quaternion is reset to identity on every step. So `Enemy.#facing()` — which reads `this.mesh.rotation.y` — is permanently `(0,0,-1)`, the FOV cone faces the wrong way, and the chase/search thrash plus the low `closedTenths` (18) both follow from it.

   Nothing has been changed yet in response to this finding. The dev server is still running in the background on port 5273 (`npx vite --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5273 --strictPort`, log at `/tmp/rifleman-dev.log`); the last full result JSON is at `/tmp/rifleman-run.json`.

9. Optional Next Step:

   Make `Enemy` own its yaw as a plain field instead of storing it on the physics-owned transform: add a `#yaw: number` (initialised to `headingAngle(second.x - spawn.x, second.z - spawn.z)`), have `#turnTowards` update `#yaw` and write it to a child group of the rifleman visual (reintroducing a `yaw`/`body` handle on `IRiflemanVisual`), and have `#facing()` derive from `#yaw`. Then drop `rotationChanged` from `playtests/enemy.playtest.json` (the observed root rotation can never change on a character body), expose a monotonic `sightings` counter in `Enemy.debug()` to replace the flip-flop-sensitive `alerted` assertion, re-run the scenario, and chase down the single console error.

   This follows directly from the failure I was mid-diagnosis on: `FAIL movement.rotation {"rotationDelta": 0}`, `FAIL component.enemy.closedTenths {"after": 18, ... "gte": 50}`, and `FAIL component.enemy.mode.value.atSteps ... {"expected": {"equals": "chase", "label": "chase"}, "pass": false, "value": "search"}`.

